    {
      "id": "f2025-final-q03",
      "number": 3,
      "points": 9,
      "stem": "The following are three attempts at evaluating the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin(x)\\cos(x)\\,dx\n$$\n\nAttempt (i):\n\nSet\n\n$$\nu = \\sin(x), \\quad dv = \\cos(x)\\,dx; \\quad \\text{thus} \\quad du = \\cos(x)\\,dx, \\quad v = \\sin(x).\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin(x)\\cos(x)\\,dx = uv - \\int v\\,du = \\sin(x)^2 - \\int \\sin(x)\\cos(x)\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can algebraically solve for the integral, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin(x)\\cos(x)\\,dx = \\frac{\\sin(x)^2}{2} + C.\n$$\n\nAttempt (ii):\n\nSet\n\n$$\nu = \\cos(x), \\quad \\text{then} \\quad du = -\\sin(x)\\,dx,\n$$\n\nand so\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin(x)\\cos(x)\\,dx = -\\int u\\,du = -\\frac{u^2}{2} + C = -\\frac{\\cos(x)^2}{2} + C.\n$$\n\nAttempt (iii):\n\nBy the double angle formula $\\sin(2x) = 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x)$, we can rewrite the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin(x)\\cos(x)\\,dx = \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\sin(2x)\\,dx = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{-\\cos(2x)}{2} + C = -\\frac{\\cos(2x)}{4} + C.\n$$\n\nWhich of these attempts is/are correct?\n\nNote: Partial credit is possible for this question.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "None of the above"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "Only (i)"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "Only (ii)"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "Only (iii)"
        },
        {
          "label": "E",
          "text": "Only (i) and (ii)"
        },
        {
          "label": "F",
          "text": "Only (i) and (iii)"
        },
        {
          "label": "G",
          "text": "Only (ii) and (iii)"
        },
        {
